<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <Description>
      The FlowX testing kit: context doubles for testing a capability as a plain class,
      and FlowTestHost, which runs a compiled flow in-process with its capabilities
      substituted, so control flow, compensation and output can be asserted directly.
    </Description>
    <PackageTags>flowx;testing;test-double;test-host</PackageTags>
    <!-- Public surface, so it is documented like any other shipped package. -->
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      This used to be Abstractions only, over a comment saying that anything needing
      FlowX.Runtime was an integration harness and belonged elsewhere. FlowTestHost
      (WP-49) is the case that comment did not anticipate and the reference is
      deliberate.

      The distinction the old comment was reaching for is real, and it is not
      "which assemblies are referenced" — it is what the thing under test is. This
      package runs the REAL engine over the REAL compiled plan in the test process:
      no server, no container, no transport, no journal, no network. That is a unit
      test of a flow, and a flow's unit is its control flow, which only the engine
      can produce. An integration harness — a real transport, Testcontainers, a
      journal — remains out of scope and out of this package (docs/23 §6).

      There is nothing to drag in that a consumer does not already have: a project
      testing its own flows references the application, and the application
      references FlowX.Runtime. Layering still points inward, and the fitness
      function in FlowX.Architecture.Tests names the new bound.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="../FlowX.Abstractions/FlowX.Abstractions.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="../FlowX.Core/FlowX.Core.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="../FlowX.Runtime/FlowX.Runtime.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
